%I A400967 #14 Oct 18 2026 17:09:36
%S A400967 4,9,16,23,28,29,24,11,-12,-47,-96,-161,-244,-347,-472,-621,-796,-999,
%T A400967 -1232,-1497,-1796,-2131,-2504,-2917,-3372,-3871,-4416,-5009,-5652,
%U A400967 -6347,-7096,-7901,-8764,-9687,-10672,-11721,-12836,-14019,-15272,-16597,-17996,-19471
%N A400967 a(n) is the number of boundary lattice points minus the number of interior lattice points of the regular tetrahedron with vertices (0,0,0), (n,n,0), (n,0,n) and (0,n,n).
%C A400967 The tetrahedron has A006527(n+1) lattice points, of which A006527(n-1) are interior and 2*n^2 + 2 = A005893(n) are on the boundary. a(n) is the boundary count minus the interior count.
%C A400967 a(n) reaches a maximum of 29 at n = 6 and is negative for n >= 9, since the interior count grows like n^3 while the boundary count grows like n^2.
%H A400967 Paolo Xausa, <a href="/A400967/b400967.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..10000</a>
%H A400967 <a href="/index/Rec#order_04">Index entries for linear recurrences with constant coefficients</a>, signature (4,-6,4,-1).
%F A400967 a(n) = (-n^3 + 9*n^2 - 5*n + 9)/3.
%F A400967 a(n) = A006527(n+1) - 2*A006527(n-1).
%F A400967 a(n) = A005893(n) - A006527(n-1).
%F A400967 G.f.: x*(4 - 7*x + 4*x^2 - 3*x^3)/(1 - x)^4.
%F A400967 E.g.f.: exp(x)*(3 + x + 2*x^2 - x^3/3) - 3. - _Stefano Spezia_, Oct 11 2026
%e A400967 For n = 6 the tetrahedron has 119 lattice points, of which 45 are interior and 74 are on the boundary, so a(6) = 74 - 45 = 29.
%t A400967 A400967[n_] := 3 - n*(n*(n - 9) + 5)/3; Array[A400967, 50] (* _Paolo Xausa_, Oct 18 2026 *)
%o A400967 (PARI) a(n) = (-n^3 + 9*n^2 - 5*n + 9)/3
%o A400967 (Python) def a(n): return (-n**3 + 9*n**2 - 5*n + 9)//3
%Y A400967 Compare A400232 (cube) and A400968 (octahedron).
%Y A400967 Cf. A005893, A006527, A342831, A400232, A400968.
%K A400967 sign,easy,new
%O A400967 1,1
%A A400967 _Henrik Arhold_, Oct 11 2026
